\begin{abstract}
We present a multi-physics simulator that couples vascular tissue dynamics, biorheological fluid flow, and astrophysical density fields within a unified Hamiltonian framework. Biological state vectors $(q_{bio}, p_{bio})$, astrophysical flux data (FITS cubes), and interaction terms are combined into a composite Hamiltonian
\[
H = H_{bio} + H_{astro} + H_{interaction}.
\]
Chaotic regimes emerging from this coupled system are quantified via Lyapunov divergence and used as a non-deterministic entropy source for cryptographic key generation. The resulting architecture, centered on the H-WAVE-CIE cryptographic spine and the Blackwell Encryption Layer (BEL), produces physics-anchored encrypted simulation streams with provenance and integrity verification. We outline the mathematical formulation, system architecture, and proposed validation pathway for laboratory evaluation.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Hamiltonian systems; chaos; bio-fluid dynamics; astrophysical flux fields; cryptography; Lyapunov divergence.

\section{Introduction}
Cryptographic systems traditionally rely on algorithmic randomness, which remains vulnerable to predictive modeling and computational advances. We propose a physics-anchored cryptographic engine in which entropy is derived from chaotic divergence in a coupled bio-fluid and astrophysical Hamiltonian system.

The H-WAVE-CIE simulator integrates vascular tissue networks, biorheological fluid solvers, voxel-level atomic state modeling, and astrophysical density fields. These domains are unified through a composite Hamiltonian whose chaotic regimes serve as the foundation for physics-anchored cryptographic key generation.

\section{System Architecture}

\subsection{Fluid--Tissue Domain}
The biological subsystem consists of:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Vascular tissue cell network
    \item Biorheological fluid solver (MASA)
    \item Multi-scale voxel bridge
    \item MASA voxel solver
    \item Heat-flux model
\end{itemize}

This subsystem yields biological state vectors:
\[
(q_{bio}, p_{bio}).
\]

\subsection{Astrophysical Domain}
Astrophysical flux fields are derived from FITS cube data, producing:
\[
(q_{astro}, p_{astro}).
\]

Cosmic density fields act as external forcing terms influencing voxel-level dynamics.

\subsection{Composite Hamiltonian}
The unified Hamiltonian is:
\[
H = H_{bio}(q_{bio}, p_{bio}) + H_{astro}(q_{astro}, p_{astro}) + H_{interaction}(q_{bio}, q_{astro}).
\]

The full state vector is:
\[
x = (q_{bio}, p_{bio}, q_{astro}, p_{astro}).
\]

\subsection{Port-Hamiltonian Formulation}
We adopt the port-Hamiltonian representation:
\[
\dot{x} = (J - R)\nabla H(x) + Bu,
\]
where $J$ is the interconnection matrix, $R$ the dissipation matrix, and $u$ external forcing.

\section{Chaotic Regimes and Key Generation}

\subsection{Lyapunov Divergence}
Chaotic behavior is quantified using:
\[
\lambda(t) = \frac{1}{t}\ln\frac{\|\delta x(t)\|}{\|\delta x(0)\|}.
\]

Positive $\lambda$ indicates chaos. Divergence trajectories are sampled to generate cryptographic keys:
\[
K(t) = f(x(t), \lambda(t)).
\]

\subsection{Chaotic Attractor Sampling}
Chaotic attractor coordinates form entropy pools. These pools are mapped into key material using:
\[
K(t_i) = g(x(t_i), \lambda(t_i)).
\]

\section{Blackwell Encryption Layer (BEL)}
The BEL module includes:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Chaotic entropy extraction
    \item Divergence-driven key generation
    \item XOR cipher module
    \item Hex-encoded encrypted output
    \item Physics-anchored provenance spine
\end{itemize}

\section{Numerical Methods}
A symplectic leapfrog integrator preserves Hamiltonian structure. Voxel-level solvers handle multi-scale interactions. FITS cube readers process astrophysical flux fields.

\section{Validation Pathway}
Laboratory validation requires:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Hamiltonian solvers
    \item Bio-fluid simulation tools
    \item FITS cube processing
    \item Chaos analysis software
    \item Cryptographic evaluation suites
\end{itemize}

Validation steps:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Reproduce Hamiltonian dynamics
    \item Confirm chaotic regimes
    \item Generate key material
    \item Evaluate cryptographic strength
    \item Verify physics-anchored integrity
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}
We present a unified Hamiltonian architecture integrating biological, fluid, and astrophysical systems for physics-anchored cryptographic key generation. The H-WAVE-CIE simulator and BEL module introduce a new class of multi-physics cryptographic systems. Laboratory validation will determine the feasibility and robustness of this approach.

